  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This summer report has a modest but genuine scholarly footprint: it has been cited twice and has documented downstream reuse in three records, including one article and one preprint. The record points to early methodological influence in heterogeneous biomedical network analysis, alongside evidence that the project\u2019s code and report materials were used by later work.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Vagelos Report Summer 2017 is a 2017 Figshare report by Michael Zietz describing research completed in the Greene Lab at the University of Pennsylvania during the Penn Vagelos Scholars Program in Molecular Life Science. The work centers on a sparse matrix implementation of degree-weighted path count over a heterogeneous biomedical network, placing it squarely in computational biology and scientific computing.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signals are still early, but they are meaningful. OpenAlex records two citations, with later use appearing in 2019 and 2023. One citing work is a PLoS Computational Biology article, and the other is a 2023 bioRxiv preprint on hetnet connectivity search. Together, these citations suggest that the report\u2019s technical ideas informed later methods for reasoning over biomedical networks.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse evidence extends beyond formal citation. The record shows three downstream reuse instances, including one article, one preprint, and one biomedical article mention in Europe PMC. That pattern is consistent with a small but visible research artifact that circulated through related computational biology and data-science communities.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader attention record is quiet: there are no event-data mentions captured, and scite reports a single mentioning citation. That does not diminish the work\u2019s value, but it does indicate that its documented reach is concentrated in scholarly reuse rather than public discussion. Available repository and data-link metadata also show the report was shared openly through Figshare and connected to related project and lab resources, supporting reuse and transparency.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is a lightly cited, openly available technical report whose influence is best seen in methodological follow-on work. Its record suggests an early-stage contribution that helped seed later research on biomedical heterogeneous networks and related computational tools.</p>",
